of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###plan-frontend-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 4\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Heert (4) with sequence ID plan-frontend-001:\\\\n\\\\n]Greetings, Director Heert. Our project is to create an HOA website. It needs a simple, user-friendly interface with the following pages: Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools (public), and Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events (protected). A login system is required for residents to access the protected content, and the 'News & Events' page must be easily updatable by a non-technical person. As Director of Frontend Engineering, your task is to create an initial plan for the user-facing website. Please report back with a proposed sitemap, a recommendation for the frontend technology stack (e.g., React, Vue, or simple HTML/CSS/JS) with justification, and a high-level overview of the user flow for logging in and accessing a protected page.\\\"\\n}\\n###plan-frontend-001###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###rep-plan-frontend-001###\\nSYSTEM CALL  \\nType: REPORT  \\n\\n{\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"plan-frontend-001\\\",\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Director Heert – Front-End Initial Planning Report\\\\n\\\\n1. Proposed Public/Protected Sitemap\\\\n   • /            → Home (summary & quick links)\\\\n   • /contact     → Contact Us (public)\\\\n   • /bylaws      → Bylaws (public)\\\\n   • /schools     → Schools – Public (public)\\\\n   • /login       → Login / Create Account (public)\\\\n   • /dues        → Dues (protected)\\\\n   • /annual-meetings → Annual Meetings (protected)\\\\n   • /news-events → News & Events (protected & editor-ready)\\\\n   • /404         → Not-Found fallback\\\\n\\\\n2. Recommended Front-End Technology Stack & Justification\\\\n   • Framework: **React with Next.js**\\\\n       – Server-side rendering (SSR) gives fast perceived load & SEO for public pages.\\\\n       – File-system–based routing fits the sitemap cleanly.\\\\n       – Easily deployable on AWS Amplify Hosting or S3 + CloudFront.\\\\n   • Styling/UI: Tailwind CSS (utility-first, rapid to theme, minimal bundle size).\\\\n   • Auth/CMS Integration: AWS Amplify libraries + Amazon Cognito.\\\\n       – Drop-in React components for sign-in/up, password reset, and session handling.\\\\n       – Built-in authorization checks for protected routes via higher-order component or middleware.\\\\n   • Simple CMS for non-technical editors: either\\\\n       a) Amplify’s Admin UI DataManager tied to a DynamoDB ‘News’ table, or\\\\n       b) Markdown files in an S3 bucket editable through Amplify’s Drag-Drop Admin UI.\\\\n     Both hide code & Git; option (a) provides structured fields (title, body, publishDate) while option (b) is near-zero cost.\\\\n   • CI/CD: Amplify console auto-build from main branch; preview builds for PRs.\\\\n   • Testing/Linting: Jest + React Testing Library and ESLint; optional Cypress for e2e.\\\\n   • Access control for protected pages handled client-side with Amplify Auth and server-side via Next.js middleware to allow SSR with token validation.\\\\n\\\\n   Simpler alternatives (vanilla HTML/CSS or Vue) were considered, but React/Next strikes the best balance between low-code admin features through Amplify, future extensibility (e.g., adding resident requests forms, payments), and strong community support.\\\\n\\\\n3. High-Level User Flow (Protected Content)\\\\n   1. Visitor navigates directly to /news-events.\\\\n   2. Next.js middleware checks Amplify/Cognito session:\\\\n        – If valid JWT present → continue; page pre-fetches News items via Amplify DataStore/GraphQL.\\\\n        – If no session → redirect to /login with ?redirect=/news-events.\\\\n   3. /login renders Amplify <Authenticator>. User signs in (or registers, triggers email verification per HOA policy).\\\\n   4